From ab90f24093b0e3fb7b9e24e3661d2ac9f3f85e7b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: deten <6029514+deten@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 15:48:05 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 03/12] gles: copy client-side vertex arrays for every draw
 call

Only glDrawArrays, and glDrawElements with client-side indices, copied
attributes that are enabled with no buffer bound. All other draws sent
the 64-bit client pointers through unchanged:

- glDrawElements with an element buffer bound,
- glDrawRangeElements (whose client-side indices were not copied either),
- glDrawArraysInstanced and glDrawElementsInstanced.

The proxy then gave the driver a pointer truncated to 32 bits, and the
driver read unrelated proxy memory or crashed in memcpy. Skia hits this
on Mali, where it prefers client-side arrays for dynamic vertex and index
data (GrGLCaps: fPreferClientSideDynamicBuffers for ARM GPUs), and draws
with glDrawRangeElements and the instanced calls.

Every draw now carries a list of the client-side attributes of the
current VAO, copied into the data arena: per-vertex arrays for the
vertex range the draw reads, per-instance arrays (divisor > 0) for the
instances it draws. The vertex range comes from the draw arguments, the
client index array, a client mapping of the element buffer, or else a new
bridge-internal call (OP_bridgeIndexRange) that reads the highest index
from the element buffer in the proxy. The proxy points the attributes at
the copies before drawing and restores GL_ARRAY_BUFFER afterwards.

Also:
- glDrawElementsInstanced decided "client indices" from the pointer value
  and sent no offset otherwise, so draws from an element buffer always
  started at offset 0; it now follows the element buffer binding and
  passes the offset.
- The copies follow the current VAO instead of the global attribute
  state, which is not updated by glBindVertexArray, and no longer copy
  one stride past the end of the array.
